        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Journal of Arts, Memory, Rituals, and Transformative Human Narratives (JAMRTHN) is a peer-reviewed academic platform committed to exploring the intricate relationships between cultural memory, artistic expression, ritual practices, and human storytelling. Published regularly under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a forum for rigorous interdisciplinary research that bridges the humanities, social sciences, arts, and cultural studies.</p>\n<p>JAMRTHN aims to advance scholarly understanding of how arts, rituals, and narratives shape, preserve, and transform human experience across historical, social, and cultural contexts. The journal encourages contributions that examine the ways collective and individual memory, performative practices, and storytelling influence identity, society, and cultural transformation.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>JAMRTHN welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, critical reviews, and practice-based scholarship across a wide range of subject areas, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Arts and Aesthetic Practices:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Visual, performing, and literary arts as vehicles for memory and cultural narratives</li><li>Intersections of art, ritual, and social transformation</li><li>Experimental and interdisciplinary artistic practices</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Memory Studies:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Collective, cultural, and individual memory</li><li>Memory in literature, performance, and visual culture</li><li>Heritage, archives, and the preservation of intangible cultural memory</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Ritual and Performance Studies:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Religious, secular, and community rituals</li><li>The role of ritual in social cohesion, identity, and transformation</li><li>Embodied and performative aspects of human expression</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Narratives and Storytelling:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Oral, written, and digital narratives</li><li>Trauma, resilience, and transformative storytelling</li><li>Intersections of narrative with social, cultural, and political contexts</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Cultural studies, anthropology, and sociology of arts and rituals</li><li>Digital humanities and innovative media in memory and narrative studies</li><li>Creative methodologies and practice-led research</li></ul>\n<p>JAMRTHN serves as a platform for scholars, artists, and practitioners to share research that illuminates the transformative power of arts, memory, rituals, and human narratives. The journal emphasizes work that bridges theory and practice, fosters interdisciplinary dialogue, and contributes to a deeper understanding of human creativity, cultural continuity, and societal change.</p>",
