        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Journal of Folklore, Mythology, and Community Heritage Preservation Studies (JFMC-HPS) is a peer-reviewed, regularly published academic journal under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing interdisciplinary scholarship on folklore, myth, and cultural heritage. The journal provides a platform for rigorous research that examines the ways in which traditional narratives, myths, and community practices contribute to cultural identity, collective memory, and heritage preservation.</p>\n<p>JFMC-HPS aims to explore the theoretical, historical, and applied dimensions of folklore and mythology while highlighting innovative strategies for preserving and sustaining community-based cultural heritage. The journal encourages research that bridges academic analysis with practice-led initiatives, fostering dialogue between scholars, cultural practitioners, and communities.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>JFMC-HPS welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, case studies, critical reviews, and practice-based scholarship across a broad range of topics, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Folklore Studies:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Oral traditions, legends, folktales, and storytelling</li><li>Rituals, customs, and traditional performance practices</li><li>Regional, indigenous, and marginalized community folklore</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Mythology:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Comparative and cross-cultural myth analysis</li><li>Myth, symbolism, and archetypes in society</li><li>Influence of myth on literature, arts, and popular culture</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Community Heritage Preservation:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Documentation and safeguarding of tangible and intangible heritage</li><li>Cultural policy, governance, and heritage management</li><li>Participatory and community-driven preservation initiatives</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary and Applied Research:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Ethnography, anthropology, and sociology of culture</li><li>Integration of digital technologies in heritage preservation</li><li>Practice-based methodologies in folklore and cultural studies</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Education and Public Engagement:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Heritage education, museum studies, and cultural awareness programs</li><li>Engagement with local communities and participatory projects</li><li>Promotion of sustainable cultural practices and knowledge transmission</li></ul>\n<p>JFMC-HPS serves as a platform for scholars, practitioners, and heritage professionals to examine, preserve, and interpret folklore, mythology, and community heritage. The journal emphasizes work that bridges theory and practice, fosters interdisciplinary dialogue, and contributes to the understanding and safeguarding of cultural traditions and communal memory.</p>",
