        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Journal of Youth, Childhood, and Learning Cultures (JYCLC) is a peer-reviewed, regularly published academic journal under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing interdisciplinary research on the experiences, learning, and development of children and youth across diverse social, cultural, and educational contexts. The journal provides a platform for rigorous scholarship examining how learning cultures, pedagogical practices, and societal influences shape the intellectual, social, and emotional growth of young people.</p>\n<p>JYCLC aims to foster critical understanding of the interplay between education, culture, and socialization, emphasizing the roles of informal, formal, and digital learning environments. The journal encourages contributions that integrate theoretical, empirical, and practice-based perspectives from education, sociology, psychology, anthropology, and related fields.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>JYCLC welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, case studies, critical reviews, and practice-based scholarship across a broad range of topics, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Childhood and Youth Studies:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Developmental, social, and cultural aspects of childhood and adolescence</li><li>Identity formation, agency, and participation in societal contexts</li><li>Cross-cultural and comparative studies of youth experiences</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Learning Cultures and Pedagogy:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Formal and informal learning environments</li><li>Educational innovations, curriculum studies, and teaching practices</li><li>Digital, experiential, and collaborative learning approaches</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Socio-Cultural and Community Perspectives:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Family, community, and peer influences on learning and development</li><li>Youth and childhood engagement in arts, media, and cultural activities</li><li>Social justice, equity, and inclusion in learning contexts</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Integration of education, sociology, psychology, anthropology, and cultural studies</li><li>Methodological innovations in youth and childhood research</li><li>Practice-led, participatory, and applied research approaches</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Applied and Emerging Research:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Policy analysis and educational reforms impacting children and youth</li><li>Community-based initiatives and youth development programs</li><li>Innovative learning technologies and digital education research</li></ul>\n<p>JYCLC serves as a platform for scholars, educators, policymakers, and practitioners to explore the dynamic relationships between youth, childhood, and learning cultures. The journal emphasizes research that bridges theory and practice, fosters interdisciplinary dialogue, and contributes to a deeper understanding of educational, social, and cultural processes shaping young people’s lives.</p>",
