        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Global Journal of Comparative Legal Systems (GJCLS) is a peer-reviewed, regularly published academic journal under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing interdisciplinary scholarship on comparative law, legal systems, and jurisprudence across different national, regional, and international contexts. The journal provides a platform for rigorous research examining the structures, functions, and evolution of legal systems, highlighting their similarities, differences, and interactions.</p>\n<p>GJCLS aims to foster critical understanding of legal diversity, cross-jurisdictional influences, and the theoretical foundations of law, emphasizing the impact of social, political, economic, and cultural factors on legal development. The journal encourages contributions that integrate doctrinal, empirical, and interdisciplinary perspectives, bridging law, political science, sociology, economics, and international relations.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>GJCLS welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, case analyses, critical reviews, and practice-led scholarship across a broad range of topics, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Comparative Legal Systems:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Civil law, common law, religious law, customary law, and hybrid systems</li><li>Comparative constitutional, administrative, and criminal law</li><li>Legal pluralism and cross-border legal harmonization</li></ul>\n<p><strong>International and Transnational Law:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Interaction between national and international legal frameworks</li><li>Human rights, trade law, environmental law, and global governance</li><li>Cross-border dispute resolution and regulatory practices</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary and Socio-Legal Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Law in social, political, economic, and cultural contexts</li><li>Integration of sociology, economics, anthropology, and political science in legal studies</li><li>Empirical, doctrinal, and theoretical methodologies</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Legal Reform and Policy Analysis:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Comparative analysis of legal reforms and legislative developments</li><li>Policy innovations, governance, and institutional design</li><li>Legal education, professional practice, and law modernization</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Applied and Emerging Research:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Case studies on cross-jurisdictional legal issues</li><li>Technology, AI, and innovation in comparative legal research</li><li>Evaluating the effectiveness of legal systems and reforms</li></ul>\n<p>GJCLS serves as a platform for scholars, legal practitioners, policymakers, and interdisciplinary researchers to explore the diversity, evolution, and global impact of legal systems. The journal emphasizes work that bridges theory and practice, encourages critical dialogue, and contributes to a deeper understanding of comparative law in a globalized world.</p>",
