        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Global Review of Musicology, Ethnomusicology, and Sound Anthropology (GRMESA) is a peer-reviewed, regularly published academic journal under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing interdisciplinary scholarship on music, sound cultures, and their anthropological, historical, and social contexts. The journal provides a platform for rigorous research examining musicological theory, ethnomusicological practices, and the cultural, social, and cognitive dimensions of sound across global societies.</p>\n<p>GRMESA aims to foster critical understanding of the role of music and sound in shaping cultural identity, social interaction, and human experience. The journal encourages contributions that integrate theoretical, empirical, and practice-based approaches, bridging musicology, ethnomusicology, sound studies, anthropology, cultural studies, and media studies.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>GRMESA welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, case analyses, critical reviews, and practice-led scholarship across a broad range of topics, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Musicology:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Historical, theoretical, and analytical studies of music</li><li>Composition, performance practices, and musical aesthetics</li><li>Cross-cultural and comparative musicology</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Ethnomusicology:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Study of music in its social, cultural, and ritual contexts</li><li>Fieldwork, ethnographic research, and community music practices</li><li>Music, identity, and globalization</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Sound Anthropology:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Anthropological study of sound, listening, and acoustic environments</li><li>Sonic ethnography, soundscapes, and auditory culture</li><li>Intersections of music, ritual, and oral tradition</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Integration of musicology, anthropology, sociology, cultural studies, and media studies</li><li>Digital and technological methods in music and sound research</li><li>Comparative and cross-cultural methodologies</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Applied and Emerging Research:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Case studies on music and sound in education, health, and social change</li><li>Preservation of intangible musical heritage</li><li>Innovative practices in performance, recording, and sound documentation</li></ul>\n<p>GRMESA serves as a platform for scholars, musicians, ethnographers, and interdisciplinary researchers to explore the dynamics of music, sound, and cultural expression. The journal emphasizes research that bridges theory and practice, fosters cross-cultural dialogue, and contributes to a deeper understanding of the social, cultural, and human dimensions of music and sound.</p>",
