        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The International Journal of Marine Policy, Coastal Resilience, and Ecological Economics (IJMP-CREE) is a peer-reviewed, regularly published academic journal under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing interdisciplinary scholarship on marine policy, coastal resilience, and ecological economics. The journal provides a platform for rigorous research examining the sustainable management of marine and coastal ecosystems, the integration of economic frameworks with ecological stewardship, and policy strategies for mitigating environmental and social challenges in coastal regions.</p>\n<p>IJMP-CREE aims to foster critical understanding of the interactions between marine ecosystems, economic policy, and community resilience. The journal encourages contributions that integrate theoretical, empirical, and applied approaches, bridging environmental science, marine policy, economics, sociology, and sustainability studies.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>IJMP-CREE welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, case analyses, critical reviews, and policy-oriented scholarship across a broad range of topics, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Marine Policy and Governance:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>National and international regulatory frameworks for marine resources</li><li>Ocean governance, fisheries management, and maritime law</li><li>Policy innovation for sustainable marine and coastal development</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Coastal Resilience:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Climate change adaptation, disaster risk reduction, and community resilience</li><li>Ecosystem-based management and nature-based solutions</li><li>Socio-ecological assessments of coastal vulnerability</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Ecological Economics:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Valuation of ecosystem services and natural capital</li><li>Sustainable resource allocation and blue economy initiatives</li><li>Integration of economic, social, and environmental objectives</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Integration of marine science, ecology, economics, policy studies, and sociology</li><li>Comparative, empirical, and modeling methodologies</li><li>Global, regional, and community-based perspectives on marine and coastal sustainability</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Applied and Emerging Research:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Case studies on marine conservation, policy implementation, and resilience-building</li><li>Innovative approaches in marine spatial planning, blue economy, and sustainable fisheries</li><li>Technology, digital tools, and data-driven solutions in marine policy and ecological management</li></ul>\n<p>IJMP-CREE serves as a platform for scholars, policymakers, practitioners, and interdisciplinary researchers to explore the dynamic relationships between marine ecosystems, governance, and sustainable development. The journal emphasizes research that bridges theory and practice, fosters global dialogue, and contributes to resilient, economically viable, and ecologically sustainable coastal and marine systems.</p>",
