        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Journal of Constitutional Futures, Democratic Transitions, and Civic Solidarity (JCFTCS) is a peer-reviewed, regularly published academic journal under Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing interdisciplinary scholarship on constitutional governance, democratic development, and civic engagement. The journal provides a platform for rigorous research examining how constitutional frameworks, democratic transitions, and civic practices shape governance, political stability, and societal resilience globally.</p>\n<p>JCFTCS aims to foster critical understanding of the complex interplay between constitutional law, democratic processes, and civic solidarity. The journal encourages contributions that integrate perspectives from law, political science, public policy, sociology, human rights, and comparative governance studies.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>JCFTCS welcomes original research articles, conceptual studies, policy analyses, case studies, and applied scholarship across a broad range of topics, including but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Constitutional Futures:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Constitutional design, reform, and interpretation</li><li>Rule of law, separation of powers, and judicial oversight</li><li>Comparative and emerging constitutional frameworks</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Democratic Transitions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Democratization processes, governance reforms, and political transitions</li><li>Electoral systems, party politics, and institutional consolidation</li><li>Challenges of authoritarianism, hybrid regimes, and democratic backsliding</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Civic Solidarity:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Civic engagement, participation, and social cohesion</li><li>Human rights, civil liberties, and inclusive governance</li><li>Community-driven governance, advocacy, and social accountability</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Interdisciplinary Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Integration of law, political science, sociology, public policy, and governance studies</li><li>Empirical, theoretical, and comparative research methodologies</li><li>Cross-national, regional, and global perspectives on constitutional and democratic developments</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Applied and Emerging Research:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Case studies on constitutional reform, democratic innovations, and civic initiatives</li><li>Analysis of policy interventions, governance strategies, and social mobilization</li><li>Evaluation of transitional frameworks fostering resilient, inclusive, and participatory societies</li></ul>\n<p>JCFTCS serves as a platform for scholars, policymakers, legal experts, civil society practitioners, and interdisciplinary researchers to explore the dynamics of constitutional governance, democratic transitions, and civic solidarity. The journal emphasizes research that bridges theory and practice, fosters international dialogue, and contributes to the development of equitable, resilient, and participatory governance structures worldwide.</p>",
