        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Journal of Policing, Security and Human Rights (JPSHR) is a peer-reviewed academic journal published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, dedicated to advancing scholarship at the intersection of law enforcement, public security, and human rights. The journal provides an interdisciplinary platform for legal scholars, criminologists, policymakers, security practitioners, and human rights advocates to engage with the evolving challenges of policing, governance, and rights protection in local, regional, and global contexts.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>JPSHR welcomes original research articles, comparative studies, case analyses, policy reviews, and critical theoretical contributions in areas including (but not limited to):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Policing and Legal Frameworks</li><li>National, regional, and international legal standards in policing</li><li>Accountability, transparency, and oversight of law enforcement agencies</li><li>Comparative models of community-oriented and democratic policing</li><li>Security, Governance, and Society</li><li>Public safety, counter-terrorism, and human security frameworks</li><li>Cybersecurity, digital surveillance, and data protection rights</li><li>The balance between state security imperatives and civil liberties</li><li>Human Rights and Policing Practices</li><li>Protection of civil, political, and socio-economic rights in law enforcement</li><li>Use of force, detention practices, and rights of vulnerable populations</li><li>Policing in conflict, post-conflict, and transitional justice contexts</li><li>Interdisciplinary Perspectives</li><li>Criminology, sociology, and anthropology of policing and security</li><li>Gender, race, class, and disability dimensions in policing practices</li><li>Ethical, philosophical, and policy debates on human rights and security</li><li>Global and Regional Challenges</li><li>International human rights law and policing standards (e.g., UN, regional treaties)</li><li>Comparative policing and security reforms across the Global South and North</li><li>Cross-border crime, migration, and global governance of security</li><li>By bridging theoretical inquiry with empirical research and policy debate, JPSHR aims to contribute to the development of policing and security practices that respect human dignity, promote justice, and uphold democratic governance.</li></ul>",
