        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Global Journal of Comparative Constitutional Studies, Human Rights, and Legal Theory (GJCCS-HRLT) is a peer-reviewed academic journal published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers. The journal provides a global platform for scholars, practitioners, and policymakers to engage with the evolving dynamics of constitutionalism, the protection of human rights, and foundational debates in legal theory.</p>\n<p>Positioned at the intersection of comparative law, jurisprudence, and governance studies, the journal aims to foster rigorous scholarship that not only examines national experiences but also interrogates transnational constitutional practices and the role of human rights in shaping democratic orders.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>GJCCS-HRLT invites original research articles, case law analyses, comparative studies, theoretical essays, and critical reviews on topics including (but not limited to):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Comparative Constitutional Studies</li><li>Models of constitutional design, amendment, and reform</li><li>Federalism, decentralization, and separation of powers</li><li>Constitutional adjudication and judicial review across jurisdictions</li><li>Human Rights and Global Justice</li><li>National and international human rights regimes</li><li>Emerging rights (digital rights, environmental rights, rights of minorities)</li><li>Human rights enforcement, monitoring, and transitional justice</li><li>Legal and Political Theory</li><li>Theories of constitutionalism, legitimacy, and sovereignty</li><li>Critical legal studies, postcolonial perspectives, and global south scholarship</li><li>Debates on rule of law, democracy, and constitutional morality</li><li>Law, Governance, and Society</li><li>Intersections of constitutional law with political economy, migration, and security</li><li>Role of courts, civil society, and media in constitutional governance</li><li>Comparative perspectives on constitutional crises and resilience</li><li>Interdisciplinary and Transnational Approaches</li><li>Philosophical, sociological, and historical perspectives on constitutional law</li><li>Global and regional organizations shaping constitutional and human rights norms</li><li>Dialogues between legal theory, technology, and cultural transformation</li><li>Through a commitment to interdisciplinary inquiry and global engagement, GJCCS-HRLT seeks to advance debates that shape contemporary constitutional thought, human rights practices, and the theoretical foundations of law in a rapidly changing world.</li></ul>",
