        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The International Journal of Legal Pluralism and Customary Law (IJLPCL) is a peer-reviewed academic journal dedicated to advancing scholarship on legal pluralism, customary law, and the interaction of multiple legal systems within societies. Published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a platform for legal scholars, anthropologists, policymakers, and practitioners to critically examine how formal, customary, religious, and informal legal systems coexist, interact, and influence governance, social norms, and dispute resolution.</p>\n<p>IJLPCL seeks to foster interdisciplinary research that integrates law, anthropology, sociology, and political science, promoting evidence-based insights into the complexities of legal pluralism, the role of customary law, and its implications for human rights, social justice, and sustainable governance. Contributions analyzing customary legal practices, plural legal systems, normative conflicts, and comparative approaches are particularly encouraged.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>The journal publishes original research articles, case studies, theoretical essays, comparative analyses, and policy reviews in areas including, but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<h4>1. Legal Pluralism and Customary Law</h4>\n<p>Interaction between statutory, customary, religious, and informal legal systems</p>\n<p>Recognition and regulation of customary law within national and international frameworks</p>\n<p>Dispute resolution mechanisms and enforcement of customary norms</p>\n<h4>2. Human Rights and Social Justice</h4>\n<p>Implications of legal pluralism for human rights protection</p>\n<p>Gender, minority, and vulnerable group rights under customary law</p>\n<p>Balancing cultural traditions with international legal standards</p>\n<h4>3. Comparative and International Perspectives</h4>\n<p>Comparative studies of plural legal systems across regions</p>\n<p>International legal frameworks and customary law recognition</p>\n<p>Cross-border conflicts and transnational customary practices</p>\n<h4>4. Governance, Policy, and Legal Reform</h4>\n<p>Integration of customary law into national legal systems</p>\n<p>Legal pluralism and public policy for social cohesion and governance</p>\n<p>Law reform initiatives and policy interventions in pluralistic contexts</p>\n<h4>5. Emerging Challenges and Interdisciplinary Approaches</h4>\n<p>Modernization, globalization, and the evolving role of customary law</p>\n<p>Interdisciplinary research linking law, anthropology, sociology, and political science</p>\n<p>Innovative solutions for harmonizing plural legal systems with human rights standards</p>\n<p>IJLPCL serves as a leading forum for scholarship that critically examines legal pluralism and customary law, fostering research, debate, and practical insights into inclusive, culturally sensitive, and sustainable legal governance worldwide.</p>",
