        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Global Review of Water Policy, Coastal Management, and Ecological Justice (GR-WPCMEJ) is a peer-reviewed academic journal dedicated to advancing scholarship on water governance, coastal resource management, and ecological justice. Published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a platform for legal scholars, environmental scientists, policymakers, and researchers to critically examine national and international frameworks addressing sustainable water use, coastal ecosystem protection, and equitable access to natural resources.</p>\n<p>GR-WPCMEJ seeks to foster interdisciplinary research that integrates law, environmental policy, ecology, and social sciences, promoting evidence-based insights into sustainable water management, coastal governance, and the legal and ethical dimensions of ecological protection. Contributions analyzing water law, coastal regulation, environmental justice, and policy innovation are particularly encouraged.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>The journal publishes original research articles, case studies, theoretical essays, comparative analyses, and policy reviews in areas including, but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<h4>1. Water Law and Policy</h4>\n<p>National and international legal frameworks for water resources management</p>\n<p>Water rights, allocation, and governance mechanisms</p>\n<p>Regulatory compliance, dispute resolution, and sustainable water use</p>\n<h4>2. Coastal Management and Ecosystem Governance</h4>\n<p>Policies for coastal zone management, marine conservation, and habitat protection</p>\n<p>Coastal resilience, climate adaptation, and disaster risk reduction</p>\n<p>Sustainable fisheries, marine biodiversity, and ecosystem-based management</p>\n<h4>3. Ecological Justice and Environmental Rights</h4>\n<p>Legal frameworks and policy approaches for environmental protection and justice</p>\n<p>Access to water, equitable resource distribution, and community rights</p>\n<p>Ethical and social considerations in environmental decision-making</p>\n<h4>4. Comparative and International Perspectives</h4>\n<p>Comparative studies of water and coastal governance across jurisdictions</p>\n<p>International treaties, conventions, and cooperative frameworks</p>\n<p>Lessons from global best practices in sustainable resource management</p>\n<h4>5. Emerging Challenges and Interdisciplinary Approaches</h4>\n<p>Climate change, pollution, and anthropogenic impacts on water and coastal ecosystems</p>\n<p>Interdisciplinary research linking law, policy, environmental science, and social justice</p>\n<p>Innovative strategies for sustainable, equitable, and resilient water and coastal management</p>\n<p>GR-WPCMEJ serves as a leading forum for scholarship that critically examines the intersection of water policy, coastal management, and ecological justice, fostering research, debate, and practical insights to enhance sustainable resource governance, environmental protection, and social equity worldwide.</p>",
