        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The International Journal of Constitutional Law, Human Rights, and Political Philosophy (IJCL-HRPP) is a peer-reviewed academic journal dedicated to advancing scholarship on constitutional law, human rights, and the theoretical foundations of political and legal philosophy. Published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a platform for legal scholars, political theorists, philosophers, and policymakers to critically examine constitutional frameworks, the protection of fundamental rights, and normative questions concerning governance, justice, and political legitimacy.</p>\n<p>IJCL-HRPP seeks to foster interdisciplinary research that integrates law, philosophy, political science, and ethics, promoting evidence-based and theoretically informed insights into constitutional design, human rights protection, and the philosophical underpinnings of political authority. Contributions analyzing comparative constitutional systems, human rights theory, judicial reasoning, and normative debates in political philosophy are particularly encouraged.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>The journal publishes original research articles, case studies, theoretical essays, comparative analyses, and policy reviews in areas including, but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<h4>1. Constitutional Law and Governance</h4>\n<p>Principles of constitutional design, separation of powers, and federalism</p>\n<p>Judicial review, constitutional interpretation, and rule of law</p>\n<p>Constitutional reforms, comparative constitutional studies, and emerging trends</p>\n<h4>2. Human Rights Law and Protection</h4>\n<p>International, regional, and national human rights frameworks</p>\n<p>Rights to equality, freedom of expression, privacy, and social justice</p>\n<p>Mechanisms for enforcement, accountability, and protection of vulnerable populations</p>\n<h4>3. Political Philosophy and Legal Theory</h4>\n<p>Theories of justice, legitimacy, and democratic governance</p>\n<p>Normative debates on rights, duties, and state authority</p>\n<p>Ethical and philosophical underpinnings of constitutional and human rights law</p>\n<h4>4. Comparative and International Perspectives</h4>\n<p>Comparative constitutional law across jurisdictions</p>\n<p>Cross-border human rights challenges and global governance</p>\n<p>Lessons from international courts, tribunals, and normative frameworks</p>\n<h4>5. Emerging Challenges and Interdisciplinary Approaches</h4>\n<p>Technology, digital governance, AI, and implications for constitutionalism and rights</p>\n<p>Interdisciplinary research linking law, political science, philosophy, and ethics</p>\n<p>Innovative strategies for promoting justice, human dignity, and constitutional accountability</p>\n<p>IJCL-HRPP serves as a leading forum for scholarship that critically examines the intersection of constitutional law, human rights, and political philosophy, fostering research, debate, and practical insights to strengthen governance, justice, and human dignity globally.</p>",
