        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The International Journal of Urban Planning, Green Technology, and Ecological Justice (IJUP-GTEJ) is a peer-reviewed academic journal dedicated to advancing scholarship on sustainable urban planning, green technologies, and ecological justice. Published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a platform for urban planners, environmental scientists, legal scholars, policymakers, and researchers to critically examine planning strategies, innovative technologies, and legal frameworks aimed at promoting sustainable and equitable urban development.</p>\n<p>IJUP-GTEJ seeks to foster interdisciplinary research that integrates urban planning, environmental law, engineering, social sciences, and public policy, promoting evidence-based insights into sustainable infrastructure, green innovation, and social equity. Contributions analyzing urban governance, climate-resilient design, ecological justice, and technological solutions for sustainable cities are particularly encouraged.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>The journal publishes original research articles, case studies, theoretical essays, comparative analyses, and policy reviews in areas including, but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<h4>1. Urban Planning and Sustainable Development</h4>\n<p>Urban design, zoning, and land use planning</p>\n<p>Smart cities, sustainable infrastructure, and resilient urban systems</p>\n<p>Policies for equitable urban development and social inclusion</p>\n<h4>2. Green Technology and Innovation</h4>\n<p>Renewable energy integration, green building, and sustainable transportation</p>\n<p>Eco-friendly construction materials and smart infrastructure technologies</p>\n<p>Innovation in climate adaptation and environmental management</p>\n<h4>3. Ecological Justice and Environmental Policy</h4>\n<p>Legal and policy frameworks promoting environmental justice</p>\n<p>Access to natural resources, pollution control, and community rights</p>\n<p>Ethical and social considerations in urban environmental governance</p>\n<h4>4. Comparative and International Perspectives</h4>\n<p>Comparative studies of urban planning, green technology, and ecological policies across regions</p>\n<p>International conventions, standards, and collaborative frameworks</p>\n<p>Lessons from global best practices in sustainable urban governance</p>\n<h4>5. Emerging Challenges and Interdisciplinary Approaches</h4>\n<p>Climate change, rapid urbanization, and environmental hazards</p>\n<p>Interdisciplinary research linking planning, law, technology, and social sciences</p>\n<p>Innovative strategies to enhance sustainable, equitable, and resilient urban ecosystems</p>\n<p>IJUP-GTEJ serves as a leading forum for scholarship that critically examines the intersection of urban planning, green technology, and ecological justice, fostering research, debate, and practical insights to promote sustainable cities, environmental protection, and social equity worldwide.</p>",
