        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The Journal of Postcolonial Justice, Indigenous Sovereignties, and Earth Law (JPJ-ISEL) is a peer-reviewed academic journal dedicated to advancing scholarship on postcolonial legal frameworks, indigenous rights, and ecological governance. Published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a platform for legal scholars, anthropologists, environmentalists, policymakers, and activists to critically examine legal, cultural, and policy mechanisms that support postcolonial justice, indigenous sovereignty, and Earth-centered governance.</p>\n<p>JPJ-ISEL seeks to foster interdisciplinary research that integrates law, indigenous studies, environmental ethics, and social sciences, promoting evidence-based insights into restorative justice, sovereignty recognition, and ecological protection. Contributions analyzing decolonization of law, indigenous governance, and Earth law approaches are particularly encouraged.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>The journal publishes original research articles, case studies, theoretical essays, comparative analyses, and policy reviews in areas including, but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<h4>1. Postcolonial Justice and Legal Reform</h4>\n<p>Legal frameworks addressing historical injustices and colonial legacies</p>\n<p>Reparations, restitution, and transitional justice initiatives</p>\n<p>Comparative studies on postcolonial legal reforms across jurisdictions</p>\n<h4>2. Indigenous Sovereignties and Rights</h4>\n<p>Recognition of indigenous governance systems, land rights, and cultural autonomy</p>\n<p>Treaty law, customary law, and international instruments supporting indigenous peoples</p>\n<p>Legal and policy strategies for empowerment and self-determination</p>\n<h4>3. Earth Law and Ecological Governance</h4>\n<p>Legal frameworks for environmental protection, sustainability, and ecological justice</p>\n<p>Rights of nature, conservation law, and climate justice policies</p>\n<p>Ethical, cultural, and legal integration of Earth-centered governance principles</p>\n<h4>4. Comparative and International Perspectives</h4>\n<p>Comparative studies of postcolonial justice, indigenous law, and environmental governance</p>\n<p>International treaties, conventions, and collaborative governance frameworks</p>\n<p>Lessons from global best practices in decolonization, sovereignty recognition, and ecological law</p>\n<h4>5. Emerging Challenges and Interdisciplinary Approaches</h4>\n<p>Climate change, resource conflicts, and indigenous resilience</p>\n<p>Interdisciplinary research linking law, social sciences, environmental studies, and ethics</p>\n<p>Innovative strategies for equitable, sustainable, and culturally informed governance</p>\n<p>JPJ-ISEL serves as a leading forum for scholarship that critically examines the intersection of postcolonial justice, indigenous sovereignties, and Earth law, fostering research, debate, and practical insights to advance social equity, cultural integrity, and ecological sustainability worldwide.</p>",
