        "Aims & Scope": "<p>The International Review of Cyber Law, Digital Governance, and Human Rights (IR-CLDGH) is a peer-reviewed academic journal dedicated to advancing scholarship on cyber law, digital governance frameworks, and the protection of human rights in the digital era. Published by Academic Research Centre Journals Publishers, the journal provides a platform for legal scholars, policymakers, technologists, and social scientists to critically examine the evolving legal, ethical, and policy challenges arising from digital technologies, online governance, and the protection of fundamental rights.</p>\n<p>IR-CLDGH seeks to foster interdisciplinary research that integrates law, technology, public policy, and human rights studies, promoting evidence-based insights into the regulation of cyberspace, digital policy innovation, and human rights compliance. Contributions analyzing cyber law, digital governance frameworks, data protection, algorithmic accountability, and socio-legal impacts of technology are particularly encouraged.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>The journal publishes original research articles, case studies, theoretical essays, comparative analyses, and policy reviews in areas including, but not limited to:</strong></p>\n<h4>1. Cyber Law and Legal Frameworks</h4>\n<p>National and international cyber regulations and compliance mechanisms</p>\n<p>Data protection, privacy law, intellectual property, and cybersecurity legislation</p>\n<p>Liability, enforcement, and judicial interpretation of cyber law</p>\n<h4>2. Digital Governance and Policy</h4>\n<p>Governance of digital platforms, AI systems, and online infrastructures</p>\n<p>Ethical, regulatory, and policy frameworks for digital transformation</p>\n<p>Public policy innovation for secure, inclusive, and accountable digital systems</p>\n<h4>3. Human Rights and Ethical Implications</h4>\n<p>Digital rights, freedom of expression, and online privacy</p>\n<p>Algorithmic bias, discrimination, and equitable access to digital technologies</p>\n<p>Balancing security, innovation, and individual rights in digital governance</p>\n<h4>4. Comparative and International Perspectives</h4>\n<p>Comparative studies of cyber law, digital governance, and human rights protections</p>\n<p>International treaties, conventions, and cross-border regulatory cooperation</p>\n<p>Lessons from global best practices in digital rights and technology governance</p>\n<h4>5. Emerging Challenges and Interdisciplinary Approaches</h4>\n<p>Cybercrime, surveillance, and emerging technological threats</p>\n<p>Interdisciplinary research linking law, technology, sociology, and ethics</p>\n<p>Innovative strategies for responsible, transparent, and human-centered digital governance</p>\n<p>IR-CLDGH serves as a leading forum for scholarship that critically examines the intersection of cyber law, digital governance, and human rights, fostering research, debate, and practical insights to advance legal certainty, digital accountability, and fundamental rights protection worldwide.</p>",
